{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790195821,"nonce":"Wzk4i6gJZDFS8MQpx2yepumLhBc=","target":"e61c43cd0f863c119e2519dad629829b01500c18303d612ac5ec620120065f68","message":"The generated `gpu` namespace covers basic compute, buffer readback, rendering\nand presentation, but does not yet cover the full wgpu feature set.\n\nThe vendored WebGPU IDL is generative input and a baseline, not the plugin's\ncontract or ceiling. Bindgen now imports dictionary inheritance, required and\ndefaulted members, typedefs, enum types and sequences. Buffer, sampler,\ntexture-view and nested texture/extent descriptors are generated end to end.\nRetained Text and Buffer fields use typed Caribou GC roots; buffer storage\nremains shared.\n\nRemaining bindgen work:\n\n- dynamic WebIDL union projections beyond explicit field overrides;\n- `record\u003cK,V\u003e` maps and nullable sequence elements;\n- callbacks, promises and interface operation generation;\n- generated descriptor-to-wgpu conversions, which remain explicit backend code.\n\nMissing portable GPU groups:\n\n- adapter features and complete limits, plus required feature/limit device negotiation;\n- explicit bind-group and pipeline layouts, dynamic offsets and binding ranges;\n- complete texture and vertex format catalogs (six and four exported now);\n- programmable constants, complete render-pass load/store state and full draw ranges;\n- query sets, timestamps, occlusion, render bundles and external textures/images;\n- error scopes, device-lost events and structured compilation messages;\n- configurable surface usage, present/alpha modes, view formats, color space and latency.\n\nwgpu 30 does not expose the IDL's texture component-swizzle and binding-view\ndescriptor members. Other wgpu extensions outside the IDL include native\nformat features, pipeline statistics, encoder/pass timestamps, unrestricted\nmapped buffers, binding arrays and non-uniform indexing, multi-draw, texture\natomics, 64-bit shaders, subgroups, mesh shaders and ray tracing. Each remains\nconditional on adapter/backend support.\n\nThe target layer is prepared separately: the crate compile-checks for Metal,\nDX12, Vulkan, Android Vulkan/GLES, browser WebGPU and Emscripten GLES. Buffer\nsizes/ranges/offsets and reported limits remain 64-bit through the Caribou ABI.\nAdd conformance fixtures as groups land rather than treating a successful\ntarget compile as complete API coverage.","files":null}]}